# AI Reflections

I've been using AI for 6 months, mostly for coding but also for general knowledge and a little image generation. I have loved it so far, it helps get a lot of personal projects done. Instead of spending lots of time reading API documentation, dealing with trial and error, and doing a ton of debugging, I can just ask it to make a feature and it will get done in 30s. I've made the Health Star Rating calculator, the child support calculator a static page, a tennis simulator, and the AFDA DB. It has helped do the research on the milk blog post. It really helps with using software—if I want to know where an option or shortcut or setting is, I just ask it instead of fumbling around in endless menus.  

It does get frustrating when the AI coding agent keeps making the same error, and I don't have the intelligence, patience, or time to debug it myself. And when doing the milk research, some of what it says is wrong—that is why I always read the AI output and also verify it. 

I now spend most of my time testing the software, something I never had the patience to do when I wrote my own software.


# AI Predictions

## Sources will become more important

AI screws up a lot and original research and  primary sources will be most important. I think there should be a set of verified knowledge that AI can draw from and combine. Humans are best at derving new knowledge and AI is great at scanning and synthesizing it.

## Programmers won't be out of a job

It helps build more software and it can replace people, but there is still some technical knowledge required. There isn't a finite amount of software that is needed; the cost of developing it has reduced, so there will be more of it. 
Maybe at some point AI will write its own language, but until then languages are human-designed, so a human should be able to understand programming languages and still be required when the AI goes awry.